<ul class="enc-list"><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/berbers"><span class="enc-name">Berbers</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Berbers, or Amazigh, are the indigenous peoples of North Africa, living across the Maghreb and the Sahara.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/berchtesgaden"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/berchtesgaden.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Berchtesgaden</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Berchtesgaden is a market town in the Bavarian Alps, near the Austrian border south of Salzburg.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/berdibeg"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/berdibeg.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Berdibeg</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Berdibeg was khan of the Golden Horde from 1357 to 1359.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/berel"><span class="enc-name">Berel</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Berel is a burial ground in the valley of the Bukhtarma river in the Kazakh Altai, in eastern Kazakhstan, with dozens of kurgans mostly of the 4th and 3rd centuries BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/berengar-i-of-italy"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/berengar-i-of-italy.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Berengar I of Italy</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Berengar I of Italy, margrave of Friuli and a grandson of Louis the Pious through his mother, was elected King of Italy when the Carolingian Empire broke up in 888.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/berengar-ii-of-italy"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/berengar-ii-of-italy.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Berengar II of Italy</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Berengar II of Italy was margrave of Ivrea who seized the Italian crown with his son Adalbert in 950.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/berengaria-of-castile"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/berengaria-of-castile.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Berengaria of Castile</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Berengaria was the eldest daughter of Alfonso VIII of Castile and Eleanor of England.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/berengaria-of-navarre"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/berengaria-of-navarre.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Berengaria of Navarre</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Berengaria of Navarre was queen of England as the wife of Richard I.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/berenice-ii"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/berenice-ii.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Berenice II</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Berenice II was the daughter of Magas, king of Cyrene, and queen of Ptolemaic Egypt.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/berenice-troglodytica"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/berenice-troglodytica.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Berenice Troglodytica</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Berenice Troglodytica, or Berenike, was a port on the Red Sea coast of Egypt founded by Ptolemy II in the third century BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/berga"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/berga.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Berga</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Berga is a town in the province of Barcelona in Catalonia, north-eastern Spain, and the capital of the Berguedà comarca in the upper Llobregat valley.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/bergedorf"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/bergedorf.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Bergedorf</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Bergedorf is a borough in the south-east of Hamburg, Germany.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/bergen"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/bergen.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Bergen</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Bergen is a port city on the west coast of Norway and the country's second-largest city.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/bergen-auf-rugen"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/bergen-auf-rugen.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Bergen auf Rügen</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Bergen auf Rügen is the main inland town of the island of Rügen in Mecklenburg-Vorpommern, Germany.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/bergen-op-zoom"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/bergen-op-zoom.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Bergen op Zoom</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Bergen op Zoom is a city in North Brabant, in the southern Netherlands, near the Scheldt estuary.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/berghof"><span class="enc-name">Berghof</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Berghof was Adolf Hitler's mountain residence on the Obersalzberg above Berchtesgaden in Bavaria.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/bergium"><span class="enc-name">Bergium</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Bergium was a hill fortress of the Bergistani, an Iberian people of north-eastern Spain, known from Livy's account of the campaign of Cato the Elder in 195 BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/bergpark-wilhelmshohe"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/bergpark-wilhelmshohe.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Bergpark Wilhelmshöhe</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">The Bergpark Wilhelmshöhe is a large landscape park on a hillside west of Kassel, Germany, laid out by the landgraves of Hesse-Kassel from the late 17th century.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/bergslagen"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/bergslagen.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Bergslagen</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Bergslagen is a historic mining region in central Sweden, stretching across parts of Dalarna, Västmanland, Örebro and Värmland.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/bergueda"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/bergueda.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Berguedà</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">The Berguedà is a comarca, or county, in the province of Barcelona in Catalonia, Spain, covering the upper valley of the Llobregat with Berga as its capital.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/berisades"><span class="enc-name">Berisades</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Berisades was one of the three kings among whom the Odrysian Kingdom was divided after the murder of Cotys I in 360 BCE, ruling the western part towards the Strymon.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/berke"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/berke.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Berke</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Berke was khan of the Golden Horde from 1257 to 1266, a grandson of Genghis Khan through Jochi.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/berkshire"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/berkshire.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Berkshire</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Berkshire is a county in southern England, west of London, with Reading as its largest town.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/berkyaruq"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/berkyaruq.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Berkyaruq</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Berkyaruq was sultan of the Great Seljuk Empire from 1094 to 1105, the eldest son of Malik Shah.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/berleburg-bible"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/berleburg-bible.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Berleburg Bible</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Berleburg Bible is an eight-volume German Bible translation with a mystical and allegorical commentary, published at Berleburg from 1726 to 1742.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/berleburg-castle"><span class="enc-name">Berleburg Castle</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">Berleburg Castle is a palace in Bad Berleburg in the Wittgenstein region of Westphalia, Germany.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/berlin"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/berlin.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Berlin</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Berlin is the capital and largest city of Germany.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/berlin-airlift"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/berlin-airlift.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Berlin Airlift</span><span class="enc-type">Crisis · 1948–1949</span><span class="enc-snip">The Berlin Airlift was the supply of West Berlin by air in 1948–1949, after the Soviet Union blockaded road, rail and canal access to the city's western sectors in June…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/berlin-conference"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/berlin-conference.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Berlin Conference</span><span class="enc-type">Treaty · 1884–1885</span><span class="enc-snip">The Berlin Conference was a meeting of European powers and the United States convened by Otto von Bismarck from November 1884 to February 1885.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/berlin-declaration-1945"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/berlin-declaration-1945.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Berlin Declaration (1945)</span><span class="enc-type">Event</span><span class="enc-snip">The Berlin Declaration of 5 June 1945 was issued by the four Allied commanders, Eisenhower, Zhukov, Montgomery and de Lattre de Tassigny, after Germany's unconditional…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/berlin-wall"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/berlin-wall.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Berlin Wall</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Berlin Wall was a guarded barrier built by the German Democratic Republic from 13 August 1961 to cut off West Berlin and stop East Germans fleeing to the West.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/bermuda"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/bermuda.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Bermuda</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Bermuda is a British Overseas Territory consisting of a group of small islands in the North Atlantic, about 1,000 kilometres east of North Carolina.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/bermudo-ii-of-leon"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/bermudo-ii-of-leon.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Bermudo II of León</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Bermudo II was King of León from 984 to 999, having first been proclaimed in Galicia in 982 against his cousin Ramiro III.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/bermudo-iii-of-leon"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/bermudo-iii-of-leon.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Bermudo III of León</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Bermudo III was king of León from 1028 until 1037, the last of the male line descended from the Astur-Leonese kings.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/bernabe-araoz"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/bernabe-araoz.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Bernabé Aráoz</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Bernabé Aráoz was an A